        "original_id": "31db0831-f864-5d6e-8752-a282251693f3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Mundo Perdido, Tikal, Teotihuacan, Valley of Mexico, State of Mexico, Mexico City\n- Concepts: architectural style, talud-tablero, Mesoamerican pyramids\n\nFacts:\n- By AD 250–300, Mundo Perdido at Tikal showed architectural influence from Teotihuacan in the Valley of Mexico.\n- By AD 250–300, Mundo Perdido at Tikal used the talud-tablero architectural form associated with Teotihuacan.\n- Teotihuacan was an ancient Mesoamerican city in a sub-valley of the Valley of Mexico in the State of Mexico, 40 km northeast of modern Mexico City.\n- Teotihuacan is known today as the site of many of the most architecturally significant Mesoamerican pyramids built in the pre-Columbian Americas.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
